{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"2ffde0ca-9959-473f-89db-298169fdbe35","status":"SUCCESS","response":"The workout generator has been updated and documentation has been written to [ANSWER.md](file:///tmp/h-yOvQWk/ANSWER.md).\n\n### Summary of Changes\n\n1. **[`gen.py`](file:///tmp/h-yOvQWk/gen.py#L10-L13)**:\n   In [`hr_target`](file:///tmp/h-yOvQWk/gen.py#L10-L13), changed the lower bound key from `\"value\"` to `\"start\"`:\n   ```python\n   def hr_target(step):\n       \"\"\"HR corridor for a step, as a percentage of the athlete's LTHR.\"\"\"\n       lo, hi = step[\"hr_pct\"]\n       return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n   ```\n\n2. **Why**:\n   - In Intervals.icu's API / FIT export engine, supplying `{\"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}` causes Garmin export to classify the target as a zone-mapped target, which makes the Garmin watch clamp the corridor to its own internal zone boundaries.\n   - Supplying `{\"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}` instructs the FIT export to treat the target as an instant-HR corridor with explicit custom lower and upper bounds, ensuring the watch displays the exact planned range rather than snapping to device zones.\n","duration_seconds":332.304467,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":254805,"output_tokens":3164,"thinking_tokens":0,"cache_read_tokens":1530552,"total_tokens":257969}}}
